<% content_for :title, "Your corner — Tondo" %>
<% content_for :description, "Where your collection lives." %>

<%# The reader's own corner (story 0017). Four states, one page.

    `here: nil` — `/you` is not one of the compass's four destinations and
    `compass_destinations` raises on any key that is not nil or one of them.
    The corner is reached by the mark in the bar, not by a fifth word in the
    row; the arithmetic for that is in `_masthead`.

    `locked:` — computed by the controller from the wall's own predicate, not
    from `@state`. A reader with no identity was very likely bounced here, and
    three of the four destinations are behind the wall that bounced them.
    Unlinked, they read as gated. Linked, they read as broken: every tap lands
    back on this page. This is the one page in the product allowed to vary its
    compass, because it is the one that is `private, no-store`. %>
<%= render "shared/masthead", label: "Your corner", here: nil, locked: @locked %>

<main class="page page--empty">
  <%# One ornament, not four. Every branch below opened with this same line —
      the `case` is about what each state SAYS, and the divider is not part of
      that. %>
  <div class="ornament" aria-hidden="true">✦</div>

  <%# Nothing may sit between `case` and the first `when` — not markup, not a
      comment, not even the whitespace text node a newline creates. ERB compiles
      this to real Ruby and anything in that gap becomes a statement inside a
      `case` with no branch yet, which is a syntax error rather than a warning.
      Hence the two keywords in one tag. %>
  <% case @state
     when :account %>
    <%# Prose, not a threshold. The other three states are standing in front of
        a decision; this one has already made it. Design review chose the
        quiet-line voice here for that reason — "signed in with Google" set at
        display size would be theatre. %>
    <p class="coda__line">Kept with your account.</p>
    <p class="coda__note">
      Signed in with <%= current_user.signed_in_summary %>. Your
      <%= pluralize(@kept_count, "kept work") %>
      <%= @kept_count == 1 ? "travels" : "travel" %> with the account, not with
      any one device.
    </p>
    <p class="coda__note">Nothing else lives here yet.</p>

    <div class="corner__doors">
      <%= button_to "Sign out", session_path, method: :delete,
            class: "caps-link signin__out", form_class: "signin__out-form" %>
      <%# The one honest dialog in the product. The wording is carried over
          verbatim from the button this replaced on `/collection`, because a
          control moving to a new screen should not quietly change what it
          warns about. %>
      <%= button_to "Delete account", account_path, method: :delete,
            class: "caps-link account__delete", form_class: "account__form",
            data: { turbo_confirm: "Deletes your account and the #{pluralize(@kept_count, "work")} you've kept. There is no undo." } %>
    </div>

  <%# ---- a registered device, no account --------------------------------
      The threshold voice: what is true, then what it costs, then the door.

      NO SIGN-IN DOORS. Google answers an embedded web view with
      `403 disallowed_useragent`, so a provider button here renders Google's
      error page inside the app. They arrive with the native transport, not
      before. The copy therefore promises nothing about accounts — describing a
      door that is not on the screen is how a reader learns not to trust the
      screen. %>
  <% when :device %>
    <% if @kept_count.zero? %>
      <p class="coda__line coda__line--ask">Nothing is kept on this phone yet.</p>
      <p class="coda__note">
        The bookmark under each artwork keeps it here, and what you keep lives
        with this phone.
      </p>
    <% else %>
      <p class="coda__line coda__line--ask">
        <%= pluralize(@kept_count, "work") %>
        <%= @kept_count == 1 ? "is" : "are" %> kept on this phone.
      </p>
      <p class="coda__note">
        <% if @doors %>
          Sign in and they move to your account. They stop belonging to this
          phone — sign out afterwards and this phone's collection is empty until
          you sign back in. Nothing is deleted.
        <% else %>
          They live with this phone and nothing else. They survive deleting and
          reinstalling the app. They do not survive a new phone.
        <% end %>
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <%# The doors, and only from a shell that can actually open one. The
        consequence is stated above rather than inside the button: a reader who
        taps has already decided, and persona 6's whole complaint is being told
        afterwards. `native: true` renders links rather than forms, because the
        shell intercepts a navigation and cannot intercept a form submit. %>
    <% if @doors %>
      <%= render "sessions/doors", native: true %>
    <% end %>

    <div class="corner__doors">
      <%= button_to "Delete this device's data", device_path, method: :delete,
            class: "caps-link account__delete", form_class: "account__form",
            data: { turbo_confirm: "Deletes this device and the #{pluralize(@kept_count, "work")} kept on it. There is no undo, and no account to restore them from." } %>
    </div>

  <%# ---- the shell before registration landed ----------------------------
      First launch in airplane mode, or a failed POST /device/registrations.
      There is no Device row, so there is nothing to count and nothing to
      delete — `DevicesController#destroy` would answer the button with a
      silent redirect. Say the true thing and offer no control rather than one
      that does nothing.

      `SessionsController#control` used to render an empty frame here, which
      was safe because an empty frame makes no claim. A whole page has to. %>
  <% when :shell %>
    <p class="coda__line coda__line--ask">This phone is still settling in.</p>
    <p class="coda__note">
      Tondo has not finished setting up on this device yet. Today's artwork is
      already here; everything else arrives with the next connection.
    </p>

  <%# ---- no identity at all ----------------------------------------------
      A web visitor, and usually one the wall has just bounced. The compass
      above marks the three doors they cannot open. This says what opens them. %>
  <% when :signed_out %>
    <p class="coda__line coda__line--ask">Sign in to keep a collection of your own.</p>
    <p class="coda__note">
      Past days, the full gallery, and works you keep — all of it opens with an
      account. No password, no sign-up form. Free, and yours.
    </p>

    <%= render "sessions/doors" %>
  <% end %>

  <%= render "shared/legal" %>
</main>
